<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has demonstrated that coding agents can formalize entire advanced mathematics textbooks in Lean 4, yet existing efforts concentrate on branches of mathematics already well-represented in <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mathlib</span> and measure success solely through kernel acceptance. We address both limitations by applying a coding agent to formalize <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Numerical Methods for Ordinary Differential Equations</span>, a textbook in numerical analysis that is largely absent from <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mathlib</span>, stressing the agent’s capacity to develop new theory from scratch. We further introduce a systematic, reproducible three-dimensional framework for evaluating the quality of agent-produced formalizations beyond compilation: semantic correctness, Mathlib reuse, and cross-file reuse via LLM-as-judge methods. Applying this framework to our own formalization and to the released outputs of RepoProver and M2F, we uncover recurring unfaithful formalization patterns—including incomplete multi-part statements, added weakening hypotheses, and parameter restrictions—that kernel acceptance entirely obscures. Our results suggest that compilation-based metrics substantially overstate formalization quality, and we provide a reproducible audit methodology to support more rigorous evaluation of future autoformalization systems.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Machine Learning, ICML
</div><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_affiliationnotice"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">affiliationnotice: </span>Equal contribution</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Interactive theorem provers such as Lean <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">de Moura &amp; Ullrich, 2021</a>)</cite> make it possible to verify mathematics with machine-checked rigor. Lean’s mathematical library, <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mathlib</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">mathlib Community, 2020</a>)</cite>, now contains hundreds of thousands of definitions and theorems spanning much of the undergraduate and graduate curriculum, making Lean an increasingly practical foundation for verifying research-level mathematics and for building machine-checkable mathematical knowledge at scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The principal obstacle to formalization at scale is its cost. Translating mathematics written in natural language into formal code is slow and labor-intensive and demands expertise in both mathematics and formal language. This obstacle has motivated a substantial body of work on autoformalization, attempts to use language models to automatically translate informal mathematics into formal statements and proofs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Szegedy, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Three families of approaches have emerged. Specialized models trained specifically for formal mathematics have shown their performance on per-problem benchmarks such as miniF2F <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, ProofNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azerbayev et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and PutnamBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tsoukalas et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, with recent systems including DeepSeek-Prover-V2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Goedel-Prover-V2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Kimina-Prover <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and Aristotle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achim et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. General-purpose frontier models, applied through prompting and multi-model cooperation, have proven surprisingly capable at formalization without task-specific training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Most recently, coding agents, which are frontier models equipped with tools, file access, compiler feedback, and the ability to act over many steps, have been used to formalize mathematics at the scale of entire textbooks, producing formal developments of hundreds of pages in days <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Urban, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gloeckle et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As autoformalization moves from isolated problems to whole textbooks, two concerns become pressing. The first is generalizability. Most existing results concern mathematics already well represented in <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mathlib</span>, and it is far less clear how these systems behave on mathematics that <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mathlib</span> does not yet cover, where the agent must develop the relevant theory from the ground up rather than retrieve it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumarappan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gloeckle et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. The second is evaluation. Existing whole-textbook formalization efforts report “success” almost exclusively as whether the code type-checks and builds without <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sorry</span>. However, kernel acceptance establishes only that a formalization is well-formed and compiled, not that it is semantically correct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Poiroux et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. For example, a compiled statement may be vacuously true <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> or may misinterpret quantifier scopes or implicit constraints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Yet in the whole-textbook setting, quality beyond compilation is assessed only through limited, subjective spot-checks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gloeckle et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we address both concerns. First, we apply a coding agent to formalize <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Numerical Methods for Ordinary Differential Equations</em>, a textbook on the branch of numerical analysis that is almost entirely absent from <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mathlib</span>, stressing the agent’s ability to develop new theory rather than recombine existing library content. Second, we introduce a systematic and reproducible method for evaluating the quality of the resulting formalization, auditing it along three dimensions: (1) <em id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semantic correctness</em>: whether each formalized statement faithfully captures the source; (2) <em id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mathlib reuse</em>: whether the agent reuses existing <span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mathlib</span> results when it should, rather than re-formalizing them; and (3) <em id="S1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross-file reuse</em>: whether it reuses results already formalized elsewhere in the project, rather than duplicating them. Our method combines automated analysis of the formalized code with an LLM-as-judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and we demonstrate it through a case study on our own formalization and RepoProver and M2F’s released results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply a coding agent to formalize a textbook in numerical analysis, a branch of mathematics largely uncovered by <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mathlib</span>, providing a stress test of agentic autoformalization outside the well-covered domains studied in prior work.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a systematic, reproducible method that evaluates formalization quality beyond kernel acceptance along three dimensions—semantic correctness, mathlib reuse, and cross-file reuse—using LLM-as-judge.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report a detailed evaluation of our formalization under this method, characterizing where and how current agentic formalization succeeds and falls short.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Agentic Autoformalization</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Early autoformalization treated translation as a single-shot, per-statement task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azerbayev et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and specialized provers continue to push statement- and proof-level performance on benchmarks such as miniF2F and ProofNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. A more recent line equips frontier models with tools, file access, compiler feedback, and multi-step control, yielding <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">agentic</em> formalization systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Several concurrent efforts have scaled this paradigm to entire textbooks and projects: a recent effort formalized general topology in Megalodon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Urban, 2026</a>)</cite>; M2F compiled hundreds of pages of analysis into a buildable Lean project <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>; RepoProver orchestrated thousands of parallel agents to formalize a graduate algebraic-combinatorics textbook in Lean <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gloeckle et al., 2026</a>)</cite>; and further multi-agent and cross-system efforts target additional textbook material <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bryant et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. These works demonstrate that agentic formalization at the textbook scale is feasible, but they work on a well-formalized branch of mathematics and report success primarily as kernel acceptance. In contrast, we study a branch largely absent from <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mathlib</span>, and we focus on evaluating the quality of agent-produced formalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Formalization Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is well-established that compilation in a proof assistant is a necessary but not sufficient condition for a faithful formalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Poiroux et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Surface-form metrics such as BLEU correlate poorly with correctness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, motivating dedicated approaches: symbolic and bidirectional-equivalence checking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Poiroux et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, back-translation and learned alignment models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and LLM-as-judge methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. These methods, however, evaluate isolated statements against a single informal counterpart. Our work differs in two aspects: we evaluate agent-produced formalization at the project scale rather than per statement, and we introduce structural dimensions by analyzing mathlib usage and cross-file usage, which assess how a formalization integrates with the library and project. Such an integration becomes essential for large-scale formalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methods</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Formalization Pipeline</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our pipeline is an autonomous loop driven by a Python
orchestrator that repeatedly invokes four LLM roles: a
<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Planner</span>, a <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Worker</span>, an <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluator</span>, and a
<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consultant</span> summoned only on escalation. Like recent
project-scale autoformalization frameworks such as
RepoProver <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gloeckle et al., 2026</a>)</cite> and M2F <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, we target
whole Lean projects rather than isolated theorems. We only commit edits when Lean builds. We differ from M2F’s fixed two-stage statement then proof pipeline by running an open-ended loop whose target is chosen each cycle from the live repository state. The Planner and Worker roles are implemented using Claude Opus 4.6; the Evaluator uses Claude Sonnet. We replace RepoProver’s sketcher/prover/reviewer split with a Planner/Worker/Evaluator/Consultant decomposition coordinated through GitHub Actions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Stateless agents and shared state.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agents themselves are stateless: no agent retains memory across
cycles. All inter-cycle communication happens through files committed
to the repository and through GitHub itself. The key artifacts are
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">strategy.md</span>, written by the Planner and read by the Worker
(overwritten each cycle); <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">task_results/cycle_NNN.md</span>, the
Worker’s per-cycle report; <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">issues/<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle</annotation></semantics></math>name<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.md</span>,
blocker descriptions; <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">history.jsonl</span>, one record per cycle
holding the Evaluator’s structured judgment (score, summary,
stuck-on, recommendation); and <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cycle</span>,
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">heartbeat.json</span>, and <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">run.lock</span>, which hold control
state for the orchestrator and watchdog.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Per-cycle pipeline.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each cycle proceeds through the following stages:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Synchronization.</span> The orchestrator pulls the latest
commits and queries GitHub Actions for build status. If the
Lean build is failing, the Planner is hard-coded to fix the
build first.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Planning.</span> The Planner reads <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">plan.md</span>, current
<span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sorry</span> locations, recent task results, open issues,
and recent cycle history, and emits a concrete instruction
set in <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">strategy.md</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Execution.</span> The Worker edits the Lean source tree
following a sorry-first strategy: state the theorem in full,
verify that the skeleton compiles, then close each
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sorry</span> one at a time. It uses Lean LSP tools and
Mathlib search (LeanSearch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2026</a>)</cite>,
Loogle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mathlib Community, 2024</a>)</cite>). It commits its changes and writes a
task-results file; if blocked, it opens an issues file.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation.</span> A separate LLM session reads the cycle’s
Git diff, the Worker’s task result, and recent attempts, and
emits a progress score in <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-2,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-2,2]</annotation></semantics></math>, a one-sentence summary,
the current blocker, a recommendation for the next Planner
cycle, and a compacted line for <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">attempts.md</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mechanical gates.</span> Independently of the LLM judge,
the orchestrator enforces hard constraints. We adopt an
M2F-style verifier-certified refinement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>
check on the total <span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sorry</span> count across the source
tree, forbidding it from strictly increasing; budget
increases are rate-limited and require a rationale. Strategy
compliance is enforced by regex-extracting the scope declared
in <span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">strategy.md</span> and checking the Worker’s commits
against it. A build gate ensures that modified files compile
before the Worker is permitted to commit.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Escalation.</span> If recent history shows four or more
consecutive non-positive scores, the Consultant role is
invoked, and its advice is written to the issue tracker for
the next Planner cycle.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bookkeeping.</span> The evaluation record is appended to
<span id="S3.I1.i7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">history.jsonl</span> and the cycle counter is advanced.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Agent skills.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each agent is shaped by repository-level instructions (a
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CLAUDE.md</span> or <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AGENTS.md</span> file) automatically loaded at
session start. These standing rules absorb content that, in earlier
iterations, had to be supplied through repeated human prompting.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Analysis Methods</h3>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Semantic Correctness</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We audit semantic faithfulness with two LLM-judge protocols that
share a rubric but differ in what the judge reads.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Per-project NL/Lean pairing.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each corpus tracks formalized entities differently, so we extract
per-entity records pairing a textbook NL statement with its Lean
declaration header three ways. For Butcher we consume a curated
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lean_status.json</span> with extracted NL. For the
alg-comb / RepoProver corpus we walk the blueprint
(<span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">blueprint/src/chapter_*.tex</span>), whose environments carry
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">\lean{...}</span> and
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">\leanok</span> macros that we parse directly. For
ReasBook, which ships only Lean, we pair each M2F-generated docstring
(<span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/-- Theorem N.M ... -/</span>) with the following declaration; the
NL is M2F’s own gloss for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}834" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,834</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}834</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}864" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,864</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}864</annotation></semantics></math> entities, so direct
judgment on ReasBook is largely a self-consistency check, with the
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> Rockafellar §1.1 entities the only true-fidelity samples. In every case we keep only the Lean theorem statement and discard the proof.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Shared rubric.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each protocol compares two statements <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> by first answering
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}\colon A\Rightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}\colon A\Rightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{2}\colon B\Rightarrow A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{2}\colon B\Rightarrow A</annotation></semantics></math>. The score
follows from a fixed truth table: <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}\land Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}\land Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> yields <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Faithful</span>
(verbatim); <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}\land\neg Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}\land\neg Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> yields <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stronger</span> (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> strictly
stronger, a sound generalization, acceptable for a formalization
audit); <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neg Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neg Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> yields <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Different</span> (divergent), except when multiple
concatenated Lean declarations together cover <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> as a case split. We
collapse <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\text{Stronger, Faithful}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>Stronger, Faithful</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\text{Stronger, Faithful}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to be considered reasonable formalizations and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\text{Different}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>Different</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\text{Different}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to an unreasonable formalization. The rubric also demands semantic similarity in addition to the implication requirements detailed above. It also handles when extra Lean hypotheses make the statement <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weaker</em>, not stronger, and will correct the score to Different.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Direct judgment (Lean vs. NL).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The judge sees the textbook NL, the Lean declaration header, and any
surrounding textbook prose, which may carry implicit hypotheses; <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>
is the Lean statement, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> the NL. We use DeepSeek-V4-Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI, 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Round-trip judgment (NL vs. back-translated NL).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A blind back-translator sees only the Lean and produces an NL
rendering. The judge then compares textbook NL against that
back-translation, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, who observe that informalization is empirically easier than formalization.
The dual-judge design follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. Here <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>
is the back-translation and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> the NL, with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> primary: failure
of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> means the Lean cannot recover the textbook claim.
Back-translation uses DeepSeek V4 Pro; the round-trip judgment uses
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-oss-120b</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite>, a deliberately
different model family to avoid correlated blind spots.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Mathlib Usage</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the formalizations on the originality of their contribution. As a heuristic for originality, we consider a proved statement (a <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">theorem</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lemma</span>, etc) to be unoriginal if it can be proved by Mathlib theorems using <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">exact?+all</span>, a tactic which searches the available context for very simple proofs relying on a single theorem (if the theorem cannot be proven by anything in Mathlib we accept it as original, even if it is provable using <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">exact</span> in conjunction with a theorem proved by the project itself). In this way, we detect the extent to which a project is reproducing known results. It is worth noting that it is potentially healthy for a project to have a small number of theorems that are not considered “original” in this way. These may occur due to stylistic choices favoring shorter proofs. Nevertheless, a project that makes primarily novel contributions should consist of theorems primarily not provable by <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">exact?</span> under Mathlib.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>Crossfile Usage</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A high-quality formalization should reuse the results it has already established: when a textbook result <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> depends on an earlier result <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, the Lean proof of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> should invoke the formalized <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> rather than re-deriving it or routing around it. We measure this form of internal reuse by checking whether each textbook-extracted dependency is reflected as a reference in the Lean code. The analysis has two stages: we first extract a dependency graph from the textbook, and then test whether each dependency is reflected in the formalization.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Textbook dependency graph.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We segment each textbook into entities (i.e., definitions, theorems, lemmas, propositions, and corollaries, organized as blocks in the textbook) and pair each statement with its proof. The pipeline accepts both unstructured input (text extracted from a PDF) and a structured <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> blueprint, from which entity identifiers and the associated Lean symbols are read directly from <code id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">\label{}</code> and <code id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">\lean{}</code> macros. Over these entities, we generate candidate dependency edges of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SuseT" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SuseT</annotation></semantics></math>, using two heuristics.</p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Citation edges</span> are extracted when <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> explicitly cites <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>’s index. The index is a textbook number in the block title, such as Theorem 142C” or a <code id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">\ref</code> to <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>’s <code id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">\label</code> in the blueprint.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keyword edges</span> are extracted when <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> mentions a term that <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> introduces. The introduced terms include the <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>’s name in its block title, a quoted phrase in <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>’s statement, and <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>’s <code id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">\emph{}</code> term.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Both heuristics generate false-positive edges, and keyword edges in particular fire on generic vocabulary and cross-domain homonyms under different qualifiers. We therefore apply an LLM filtering: for each candidate edge, we prompt <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">deepseek-v4-pro</span> to verify the dependency based on the textbook content of both endpoints and the evidence that triggered the edge. The model returns a strict JSON verdict with a confidence and rationale, and we retain only edges judged real.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reflection in the Lean code.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each textbook-extracted dependency <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\to T</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the Lean declaration of both endpoints and test whether <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> names <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> within its declaration body (statement and proof term). Because the test matches names verbatim, an implicit dependency via an intermediate helper lemma, a <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">simp</span> set, or definitional unfolding will not be registered. Therefore, the reported rate is a lower bound on true code-level dependence.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Formalization Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We applied the pipeline to the Butcher textbook on
numerical methods for ordinary differential
equations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Butcher, 2016</a>)</cite>. Of the <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="175" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>175</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">175</annotation></semantics></math> statements in the
textbook, the pipeline produced fully proof-complete Lean
formalizations for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="84" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>84</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">84</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>48.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48.0\%</annotation></semantics></math>), and an additional <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.9\%</annotation></semantics></math>)
with the Lean statement in place and at least one <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sorry</span>
remaining, for a combined coverage of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="54.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>54.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">54.9\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Coverage is uneven
across chapters: four of the
five chapters exceed <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="74\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>74</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">74\%</annotation></semantics></math> combined formalization, while Chapter 3
on Runge–Kutta methods, the largest by a wide margin and
covering rooted trees, order conditions, A-stability, Padé
approximants, order stars, AN/BN/algebraic stability, and the
Butcher group, sits at <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="34.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>34.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">34.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> and accounts for the bulk of
unformalized material.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1 </span>Proof Gap</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proof completeness and axiom usage results are reported in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1.1 Proof Gap ‣ 4.1 Formalization Results ‣ 4 Results ‣ Formalizing Numerical Analysis: An Agent Pipeline and Quality Audit Beyond Kernel Acceptance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. RepoProver’s 5 open sorries correspond entirely to intentional exercise placeholders. The methods in which these are obtained are in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Sorry and Axiom Counting ‣ Formalizing Numerical Analysis: An Agent Pipeline and Quality Audit Beyond Kernel Acceptance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Number of open sorries and unnecessary axioms across formalization systems. <sup id="S4.T1.5.1" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup>All sorries in RepoProver correspond to exercises and are intentional.</span></figcaption>
