</figure>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1 </span>Qualitative results</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the direct judge, two of the authors with Lean expertise
drew a stratified random sample of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> OpenMath entities, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>
judged faithful or stronger (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OK</span>) and
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> judged divergent (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DIV</span>) with seed <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>42</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42</annotation></semantics></math>, and
re-graded each one independently using the same <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}/Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}/Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> rubric the
judge was given. Disagreements were resolved by discussion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We agreed with the judge on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10/10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10/10</annotation></semantics></math> OK verdicts and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8/10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8/10</annotation></semantics></math> DIV
verdicts, for <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18/20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18/20</annotation></semantics></math> overall. The eight agreed-DIV cases were divergences from the textbook, falling into recurring
patterns documented below. The two disagreements both went in the
same direction; the judge marked DIV entities we considered
faithful, both times, because the LLM didn’t understand standard Mathlib convention.
Additionally, we inspected the four entities marked Stronger by the
judge, and found that two are sound generalizations
exhibiting interesting strengthenings.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Divergence: incompleteness.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The most common DIV pattern in our sample is the Lean formalization
covering only part of a multi-part textbook statement. Definition
312A defines three quantities simultaneously, elementary weights
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, internal weights <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, and derivative weights
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Phi_{i}D)(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Phi_{i}D)(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, through four coupled equations; the Lean
formalization defines only <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">derivativeWeight</span>. Similarly,
Definition 356B defines both DJ-reducibility and the explicit
construction of the reduced method; the Lean defines only the
reducibility predicate. These cases would require the case-split
exception to recover, which applies only when multiple Lean
declarations together cover the NL domain — a single declaration omitting a conjunct is correctly flagged Different.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Divergence: added hypotheses.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A second recurring pattern is Lean formalizations that add
hypotheses absent from the textbook, making the Lean statement
strictly weaker. Theorem 514A is stated in Butcher without normwise
hypotheses, while the Lean version requires an additional
<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">h_norm_obligation</span> bounding the matrix norm. Theorem 520B
adds invertibility (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">h_inv</span>) and stage-equation
(<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">hY_stage</span>) hypotheses not present in the NL. Definitions
530B and 530C similarly add explicit-method and Lipschitz hypotheses
to a statement the textbook gives unconditionally. The rubric
correctly treats these as DIV, since the added hypothesis breaks
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (Lean <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> NL) on the full textbook domain.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Divergence: parameter restriction.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem 550A states a result for an arbitrary <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> doubly
companion matrix. The Lean formalization proves seven separate
theorems for <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1,\ldots,7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1,\ldots,7</annotation></semantics></math>. The textbook’s universal claim is
not recovered by any finite union of these cases, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> fails
for <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math>. The judge flags this as Different with the reason
“Lean only covers <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1\ldots 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1\ldots 7</annotation></semantics></math>, not arbitrary <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,” which is the
correct application of the rubric.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generalization: Skolemizing existentials.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Stronger verdicts surfaced a complementary pattern in which the
agent strengthens a textbook claim by computing witnesses
explicitly. Theorem 406C states that “there exist constants <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C,D</annotation></semantics></math>
such that” a global-error recurrence bound holds. The Lean version
<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">globalError_recurrence_bound_textbook</span> replaces the
existential with explicit closed-form expressions for <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>
in terms of the method coefficients and Lipschitz data. The Lean
statement implies the textbook one (instantiate <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C,D</annotation></semantics></math> from the
formulas) but not the reverse, so <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> holds while <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> does not
— this is correctly classified as Stronger.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generalization: redundant hypotheses removed.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem 523A states an energy identity for general linear methods,
prefacing it with the assumption that the stability matrix <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>,
weight matrix <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and diagonal scaling <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> are positive
semi-definite. The Lean formalization
<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">algebraicStability_identity</span> drops these PSD hypotheses
and states the identity as an unconditional algebraic equality.
This is mathematically correct — the equation is a linear-algebra
identity that does not require PSD — and represents the agent
recognizing that the textbook’s hypotheses are stronger than the
identity actually needs. The judge correctly flagged this as Different.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Disagreement: Skolemized definitions.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The judge failed to recognize
Skolemization in the opposite direction: when the textbook uses an
existential and the Lean exposes the witness as a parameter rather
than binding it inside an <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∃</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists</annotation></semantics></math>, the judge reads the parameter
as an added hypothesis.
Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Disagreement: Skolemized definitions. ‣ 4.2.1 Qualitative results ‣ 4.2 Semantic Correctness ‣ 4 Results ‣ Formalizing Numerical Analysis: An Agent Pipeline and Quality Audit Beyond Kernel Acceptance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the canonical case.
Butcher’s Definition 142A defines power-boundedness as the existence
of a constant <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> bounding all powers of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. The Lean
<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PowerBounded M a</span> takes the bound as an explicit parameter,
and the textbook predicate is recovered as
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists M,\texttt{PowerBounded}\ M\ a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">PowerBounded</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists M,\texttt{PowerBounded}\ M\ a</annotation></semantics></math>, a standard Mathlib idiom
for existentially defined properties. The judge flagged this as Different
with the reason “Lean requires explicit <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, NL requires existence
of some <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>,” reading the parameter binding as an added hypothesis.
We consider this Faithful: under the Skolemized reading both <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
hold, and exposing witnesses as parameters is ubiquitous in Mathlib.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.F1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="S4.F1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">NL (Butcher 142A).<span id="S4.F1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> A square matrix <math id="S4.F1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S4.F1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stable</em>
(“power-bounded”) if there exists a constant <math id="S4.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S4.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A^{n}\|\leq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A^{n}\|\leq C</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0,1,2,\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0,1,2,\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.F1.4" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="S4.F1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Lean.<span id="S4.F1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><pre id="S4.F1.5" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_figure_panel ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">
def PowerBounded
    {A : Type*} [SeminormedRing A]
    (M : R) (a : A) : Prop :=
  forall k : N, ||a ^ k|| &lt;= M
</pre></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.F1.6" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="S4.F1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Judge:<span id="S4.F1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Different.  </span>Experts:<span id="S4.F1.6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Faithful.
</span></span></p></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.F1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Judge–human disagreement on Butcher’s definition of
power-boundedness. The judge reads the parameter <math id="S4.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as an added
hypothesis; under the standard Mathlib Skolemization convention,
the textbook predicate is recovered by <math id="S4.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists M,\texttt{PowerBounded}\ M\ a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">PowerBounded</mtext><mo lspace="0.460em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.460em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists M,\texttt{PowerBounded}\ M\ a</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implications.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The audit suggests the direct judge is largely reliable: <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> verdicts in the stratified sample matched ours, and the eight
DIV cases we agreed with correspond to formalization gaps
that kernel acceptance would not have appeared — partial coverage
of multi-part statements, added hypotheses, and parameter
restrictions. The judge’s errors cluster around a single failure
mode — confusion about the direction of implication when the
formalization re-parameterizes existentials, removes hypotheses, or
states only one half of an iff — present in both the Skolemized
DIV cases. The headline DIV
rate in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Semantic Correctness ‣ 4 Results ‣ Formalizing Numerical Analysis: An Agent Pipeline and Quality Audit Beyond Kernel Acceptance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> slightly over-counts
genuine divergence.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Mathlib Usage</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All three repositories had low Mathlib overlap rates (only a small number of the proofs were replaceable with <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">exact?</span>), indicating that they build off of Mathlib while still being composed primarily of novel contributions.</p>
</div>
