<td id="S4.T4.5.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16/33" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">16</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">33</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16/33</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T4.5.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  (<math id="S4.T4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>48</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48\%</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1164/2557" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1164</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2557</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1164/2557</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T4.5.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  (<math id="S4.T4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>45</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45\%</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply the analysis of Section <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS3" title="3.2.3 Crossfile Usage ‣ 3.2 Analysis Methods ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Formalizing Numerical Analysis: An Agent Pipeline and Quality Audit Beyond Kernel Acceptance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.3</span></a> to two independently produced formalizations: our own formalization of <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Numerical Methods for Ordinary Differential Equations</span> and the publicly released formalization of the algebraic combinatorics blueprint of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gloeckle et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, which allows us to validate the method on an externally produced project-scale corpus.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dependency validation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The edge heuristics over-generate, and the LLM filter removes a large fraction of candidates: of <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="136" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>136</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">136</annotation></semantics></math> candidate edges for our textbook, it retains <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="77" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>77</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">77</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="57\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>57</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">57\%</annotation></semantics></math>) as real dependencies; of <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7{,}090" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7,090</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7{,}090</annotation></semantics></math> candidates the RepoProver’s textbook, it retains <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2{,}603" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2,603</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2{,}603</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="37\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>37</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">37\%</annotation></semantics></math>). The filter treats the two edge types very differently. It keeps almost all explicit-citation edges, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="92\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>92</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">92\%</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="33/36" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>33</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">33/36</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="96\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>96</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">96\%</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="701/728" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>701</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>728</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">701/728</annotation></semantics></math>) respectively in the two textbooks, and rejects most keyword edges, keeping only <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="43\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>43</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">43\%</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="43/99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>43</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">43/99</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\%</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}876/6{,}335" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1,876</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6,335</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}876/6{,}335</annotation></semantics></math>). An explicit cross-reference in the text is nearly always a real dependency, whereas a shared introduced term is more often an ambient co-mention or a cross-domain homonym with a different qualifier.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reuse rates.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Restricting to validated dependencies whose endpoints are both formalized, fewer than half are reflected as name-level references in the Lean code: <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>48</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48\%</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16/33" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>33</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16/33</annotation></semantics></math>) for our formalization and <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>45</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45\%</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}164/2{,}557" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1,164</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2,557</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}164/2{,}557</annotation></semantics></math>) for the RepoProver formalization (Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Crossfile Usage ‣ 4 Results ‣ Formalizing Numerical Analysis: An Agent Pipeline and Quality Audit Beyond Kernel Acceptance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.1 </span>Qualitative Results</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To understand the unreflected edges, we manually inspected a sample of 30 of them and classified the cause of non-reflection into three categories (counts in brackets).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(1) Discharged via a library lemma <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[24]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>24</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[24]</annotation></semantics></math>.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof reuses a Mathlib result that already encapsulates the target, so the project entity is never named. For example, the corollary <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cor.det.sig-row-col</span> (determinant under row/column permutation) depends on the project’s sign-properties proposition <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">prop.perm.sign.props</span>, but its Lean proof discharges this through the Mathlib lemma <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Matrix.det_permute</span> (Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ (1) Discharged via a library lemma [24]. ‣ 4.4.1 Qualitative Results ‣ 4.4 Crossfile Usage ‣ 4 Results ‣ Formalizing Numerical Analysis: An Agent Pipeline and Quality Audit Beyond Kernel Acceptance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><pre id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_centering ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">
-- cor.det.sig-row-col
-- depends on prop.perm.sign.props
theorem det_permute_rows
    (A : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) K)
    (t : Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) :
    (A.submatrix t id).det =
        Equiv.Perm.sign t * A.det :=
  Matrix.det_permute t A
</pre>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A category-(1) unreflected edge. The corollary’s dependency on the project’s
sign-properties proposition (<span id="S4.F2.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">prop.perm.sign.props</span>) is discharged through the
Mathlib lemma <span id="S4.F2.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Matrix.det_permute</span>, so the proposition’s declarations are never
named in the corollary’s proof. Non-reflection here reflects <em id="S4.F2.8.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">library reuse</em>, not a
missing dependency.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(2) Independent reproof <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[4]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[4]</annotation></semantics></math>.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof establishes the result by a different route than the textbook. The corollary <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cor.fps.anti-newton-binom-2</span> (<math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+x)^{-n}=\sum_{k}\binom{-n}{k}x^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+x)^{-n}=\sum_{k}\binom{-n}{k}x^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>) does not invoke the proposition it cites; instead, <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fps_onePlusX_pow_neg’</span> proves the identity directly via binomial-series manipulation and power-series inversion.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(3) Non-load-bearing citation <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[2]</annotation></semantics></math>.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The textbook reference is for identification or context, not a logical dependency. In <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lem.det.cauchy-poly-dvd-y</span>, the cited <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lem.det.cauchy-poly-dvd-x</span> only names the Cauchy matrix <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(m)</annotation></semantics></math>; the <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>-divisibility proof does not use the <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>-divisibility result, and the Lean proof correctly does not reference it.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Compilation is Not Correctness</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The divergence rates we observe, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>42</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42\%</annotation></semantics></math> for OpenMath, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\%</annotation></semantics></math> for
M2F, and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18\%</annotation></semantics></math> for RepoProver by direct judgment, are consistent
across three independent systems, two judgment protocols, and two model
families, and they persist even when sorry and axiom counts are zero.
OpenMath produces zero open <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sorry</span>s and zero user axioms yet
<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>42</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42\%</annotation></semantics></math> divergence; RepoProver is effectively sorry-free yet <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18\%</annotation></semantics></math> of
its statements diverge from their NL sources. The field currently treats low sorry and axiom
counts as quality proxies and is thus entirely blind
to these failures. The
<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\!24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\!24</annotation></semantics></math>-point gap between RepoProver and OpenMath plausibly reflects the effect of
mathlib coverage of the target domain, with M2F’s intermediate result being
consistent with its target being a mix of field well covered and not covered by mathlib. The <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="72" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>72</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">72</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="78\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>78</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">78\%</annotation></semantics></math> agreement
between direct and round-trip judgment using different model families
reduces the risk that the divergence rates are judge artifacts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Recurring Divergence Patterns</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The qualitative audit (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18/20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18/20</annotation></semantics></math> human–judge agreement) reveals that
divergences fall into three recurring structural patterns. Incompleteness, a single Lean declaration covering only part of a multi-conjunct
textbook statement, is the most common: Definition 312A defines
three quantities through four coupled equations but the Lean formalizes
only <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">derivativeWeight</span>. Hypothesis addition makes the Lean
strictly weaker than the textbook: Theorem 514A gains
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">h_norm_obligation</span>, Theorem 520B gains <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">h_inv</span> and
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">hY_stage</span>, none of which appear in the NL. Parameter
restriction is the most mathematically consequential: Theorem 550A
states a universal result for arbitrary <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrices but the
Lean proves only <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1,\ldots,7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1,\ldots,7</annotation></semantics></math>, and no finite union recovers the
universal claim. All three patterns are invisible to kernel acceptance
and each maps to a concrete remediation target in the pipeline.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Limitations and Future Work</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The LLM judge has a known, well-localized failure mode: it reads
Lean parameter bindings as added hypotheses rather than recognizing
standard Mathlib Skolemization idioms (Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Disagreement: Skolemized definitions. ‣ 4.2.1 Qualitative results ‣ 4.2 Semantic Correctness ‣ 4 Results ‣ Formalizing Numerical Analysis: An Agent Pipeline and Quality Audit Beyond Kernel Acceptance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>),
causing conservative over-counting of divergence. The cross-file
reflection test is a lower bound, since implicit dependencies via
<span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">simp</span> sets or definitional unfolding are not registered. The
M2F semantic evaluation is largely a self-consistency check because
the NL source is M2F’s own generated docstring rather than the original
textbook for all but 30 entities. Future work should extend the audit
to more textbooks and domains, standardize the Skolemization handling
in the rubric, and close the gap between mathlib premise-selection tools, which treat reuse as an input to proof search, and reuse
measurement as an output quality metric for generated formalizations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A further limitation concerns coverage of the semantic evaluation itself.
A small number of entities were not evaluated due to parsing failures
during NL/Lean record extraction and intermittent API errors during
judgment. These dropped entities are not missing at random: parsing
failures tend to cluster on syntactically complex statements, and
unevaluated entities reduce the pool over which agreement is computed,
which can deflate the reported agreement percentages. Future work should
add retry logic and fallback parsers to ensure full coverage.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Impact Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presents work whose goal is to advance the field of Machine
Learning. There are many potential societal consequences of our work, none
which we feel must be specifically highlighted here.</p>
</div>
</section>
